This is one of the most frequently asked Java interview questions.

throw	                                            throws
Throws an exception object explicitly.      	Declares possible exceptions in a method signature.
Used inside a method or block.              	Used in a method declaration.
Followed by an exception object.	            Followed by exception class names.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
exp of throw :--------------

public class Main {
    static void checkAge(int age) {
        if (age < 18) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException(
                "Age must be at least 18"
            );
        }

        System.out.println("Eligible");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        checkAge(15);
    }
}
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

exp of Throws :-------------

import java.io.IOException;

public class Main {
    static void readFile() throws IOException {
        throw new IOException("File could not be read");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            readFile();
        } catch (IOException e) {
            System.out.println(e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
throws IOException declares that the method may pass an IOException to its caller. It does not itself throw the exception.










